need to decide. The examples don't show output. Possibly the order is based on the original items list order. In delta debugging, order matters for splitting. But output order likely is arbitrary. We'll output in the order they appear in items after reduction.\n\nGiven the problem is from a coding challenge, they likely expect a simple deterministic algorithm.\n\nLet's design the solve function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['items']\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n    unresolved = set(case['unresolved_without'])\n    # we'll maintain a set of candidate items that are still in the potential minimal set.\n    # Start with all items.\n    current_set = set(items)\n    # but we know required and unresolved must be in any failing set, so we can start with them.\n    # However, the initial set must include all items? Actually to simulate delta debugging, we start with full set and try to remove.\n    # But we can also start with the union of required and unresolved (if that set fails). It should fail because it contains required and unresolved. Then we can try to add other items? Actually we want to remove items, not add. So we start with full set, but we know we cannot remove required or unresolved. So we iterate over items that are not required and not unresolved, and try to remove them one by one. Since removal doesn't affect failure if the remaining still contains required and unresolved, we can remove any such item. But wait: the failure condition only requires required and unresolved to be present; all other items are irrelevant. So we can remove all non-essential items and still fail. So the minimal failing set is exactly the union of required and unresolved. So the greedy removal will remove all others. So the minimal failure set is union of required and unresolved. That seems too simple. However, maybe there's a twist: the test function behavior when you have only required and unresolved might be pass because some other item is neede